WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.852 --> 00:00:05.531
Outside your field you can't look at the work and judge how far along it is.

2
00:00:06.516 --> 00:00:09.872
So measure what is done or not done, with evidence you can see.

3
00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:14.248
A milestone is a point where a clear result has to exist.

4
00:00:15.302 --> 00:00:18.471
For example: interface tested with real orders.

5
00:00:19.168 --> 00:00:20.817
Or: switchgear energised.

6
00:00:21.871 --> 00:00:24.495
It's a yes or a no, not a percentage.

7
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:28.527
Percent complete, the share said to be finished,

8
00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:31.093
is the weakest measure outside your field.

9
00:00:32.183 --> 00:00:35.829
It's an opinion, and it tends to stall near 90 percent.

10
00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:40.286
Budget spent tells you about money, not about progress.

11
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:46.143
So count finished milestones, and only count one when its evidence exists.

12
00:00:47.287 --> 00:00:50.665
A signed test report, a demo, an approved drawing.

13
00:00:52.043 --> 00:00:55.398
Acceptance criteria are the conditions to be accepted,

14
00:00:55.561 --> 00:00:57.384
written before the work starts.

15
00:00:58.366 --> 00:01:04.171
For example: the label prints in under 3 seconds, and all 12 fields match the order.

16
00:01:05.241 --> 00:01:07.540
Agree them with the expert and the user.

17
00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:10.535
Then done isn't argued about later.

18
00:01:11.553 --> 00:01:16.290
The definition of done is the shared quality standard to count as finished: tested,

19
00:01:16.406 --> 00:01:17.788
documented, reviewed.

20
00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:20.459
It comes from the Scrum Guide.

21
00:01:21.253 --> 00:01:25.514
Most teams keep it as a short checklist; it works on any project.

22
00:01:26.847 --> 00:01:30.690
Automation and electrical work have two formal milestones.

23
00:01:31.462 --> 00:01:35.769
The factory acceptance test, the FAT, tests a machine at the supplier,

24
00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:36.640
before shipping.

25
00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:42.699
The site acceptance test, the SAT, follows after installation at the client.

26
00:01:43.754 --> 00:01:46.459
A FAT passed with open points is common.

27
00:01:47.165 --> 00:01:49.835
Give that list owners and dates before you ship.

28
00:01:50.897 --> 00:01:54.531
Demos are your best meter: regular, short, real data.

29
00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:58.890
One every two weeks shows more than ten status reports.

30
00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:02.495
A typical failure: months without a milestone.

31
00:02:03.201 --> 00:02:07.206
A rough rule: something to check at least every two to four weeks.

32
00:02:08.208 --> 00:02:10.553
So ask: what can you show me in two weeks?

33
00:02:11.859 --> 00:02:13.566
An MES project.

34
00:02:14.291 --> 00:02:16.985
The supplier reports 70 percent complete.

35
00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:19.369
You rebuild the progress view.

36
00:02:20.368 --> 00:02:25.209
Step one: list the six milestones, from design approved to the go-live rehearsal.

37
00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:31.141
Step two: the evidence for each, from a signed design to a rehearsal report.

38
00:02:32.241 --> 00:02:34.307
Step three: count what has evidence.

39
00:02:35.029 --> 00:02:36.434
Only 1 and 2.

40
00:02:37.182 --> 00:02:40.247
Milestone 3 is almost done, without a check.

41
00:02:41.302 --> 00:02:45.563
Step four: report 2 of 6 with evidence, the third in two weeks.

42
00:02:46.278 --> 00:02:48.437
The 70 percent was effort spent.

43
00:02:49.821 --> 00:02:51.249
You rarely have certainty.

44
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:55.706
Decide well with what you have, and know when it isn't your decision.

45
00:02:56.801 --> 00:03:00.063
Is it a reversible decision, one you can undo at low cost?

46
00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.571
In his letter for 2015,

47
00:03:03.757 --> 00:03:08.146
Jeff Bezos called irreversible decisions one-way doors: no way back.

48
00:03:08.896 --> 00:03:11.032
Reversible ones are two-way doors.

49
00:03:12.041 --> 00:03:14.769
Take two-way doors fairly fast, and learn.

50
00:03:15.464 --> 00:03:21.826
His letter for 2016: about 70 percent of the information you want usually suffices;

51
00:03:21.954 --> 00:03:24.009
waiting for 90 is too slow.

52
00:03:25.014 --> 00:03:29.948
A one-way door: ordering switchgear with a long lead time, the time until delivery,

53
00:03:30.076 --> 00:03:31.841
or shutting down the old system.

54
00:03:32.958 --> 00:03:37.892
There you slow down: more evidence, a second opinion, and the sponsor decides.

55
00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:43.486
Escalation is taking a decision or problem to someone with more authority,

56
00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:45.448
because it is beyond yours.

57
00:03:46.462 --> 00:03:47.530
It is not failure.

58
00:03:48.258 --> 00:03:49.593
It is the system working.

59
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:55.156
Escalate when it's outside your mandate, when experts are stuck, or when goal,

60
00:03:55.284 --> 00:03:56.898
budget or date is at risk.

61
00:03:57.925 --> 00:04:03.231
Escalate early, with options, not just a problem: two options, what each costs,

62
00:04:03.393 --> 00:04:04.682
and my recommendation.

63
00:04:06.049 --> 00:04:09.695
A decision memo makes it concrete: one page with question,

64
00:04:09.869 --> 00:04:14.466
options, criteria, expert advice, your recommendation and deadline.

65
00:04:15.466 --> 00:04:19.471
It forces you to translate the technical part into consequences.

66
00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.698
Then state the decision clearly, without apology.

67
00:04:24.377 --> 00:04:27.651
In Jeffrey Pfeffer's words: show up in powerful fashion.

68
00:04:28.624 --> 00:04:32.618
Say it calmly and firmly, and don't undercut it with sorry, maybe.

69
00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:36.138
Typical failures: escalating too late,

70
00:04:36.301 --> 00:04:39.180
with no options left; without a recommendation,

71
00:04:39.273 --> 00:04:43.069
so the sponsor does your thinking; reopening decisions every week.

72
00:04:44.106 --> 00:04:47.740
Ask: if we can't undo this, who decides, and by when?

73
00:04:48.453 --> 00:04:50.613
If we can, what does a week's wait cost?

74
00:04:51.972 --> 00:04:53.063
A grid project.

75
00:04:53.763 --> 00:04:59.173
The switchgear has a 26-week lead time; the final load figures come in 4 weeks.

76
00:04:59.898 --> 00:05:02.580
Waiting slips energising by about a month.

77
00:05:03.633 --> 00:05:05.909
Step one: ordering is a one-way door.

78
00:05:06.662 --> 00:05:08.136
Cancelling costs a lot.

79
00:05:09.245 --> 00:05:11.137
Step two, the grid engineer:

80
00:05:11.335 --> 00:05:16.118
the figures only change the switchgear if the load rises by more than 30 percent.

81
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.331
Step three, ask the factory: how likely is that?

82
00:05:21.095 --> 00:05:22.152
Very unlikely.

83
00:05:23.165 --> 00:05:27.600
Step four, a decision memo to the sponsor: order now on the current sizing,

84
00:05:27.774 --> 00:05:30.897
risk named, or wait 4 weeks and slip a month.

85
00:05:31.622 --> 00:05:33.294
Recommendation: order now.

86
00:05:34.373 --> 00:05:36.637
Step five: the sponsor decides.

87
00:05:37.352 --> 00:05:39.546
You record it and announce it plainly.
